numbered="true"
show-heading="true"
show-metadata="false"
expanded="true"
root="false"><fr:frontmatter><fr:anchor>350</fr:anchor><fr:taxon>Proof</fr:taxon><fr:addr>#219</fr:addr><fr:route>unstable-219.xml</fr:route><fr:date><fr:year>2024</fr:year><fr:month>5</fr:month><fr:day>11</fr:day></fr:date><fr:date><fr:year>2024</fr:year><fr:month>5</fr:month><fr:day>11</fr:day></fr:date><fr:authors><fr:author><fr:link
href="hejohns.xml"
type="local"
addr="hejohns">Johnson He</fr:link></fr:author></fr:authors><fr:parent>hejohns-000E</fr:parent></fr:frontmatter><fr:mainmatter><fr:p>
        We use the same notation and definitions of <fr:tex>η, ϵ, \overline {F}, \overline {U}</fr:tex> as <fr:link
href="hejohns-000D.xml"
type="local"
addr="hejohns-000D">the
        explicit proof of the precursor lemma</fr:link>.
        The definition of <fr:tex>\overline {F}, \overline {U}</fr:tex> in <fr:link
href="sub-0007.xml"
type="local"
addr="sub-0007">the short proof</fr:link>
        presupposes that <fr:tex>U</fr:tex> is a functor, so we're scrupulous to avoid
        circularity.
    </fr:p><fr:p>
        This proof is rather low tech and is lifted straight from <fr:link
href="street-2012.xml"
type="local"
addr="street-2012">the
        paper</fr:link>. It's also how you'd attack <fr:link
href="hejohns-0007.xml"
type="local"
addr="hejohns-0007">the
        precursor</fr:link> head-on. The utility is that it holds in
        enriched categories.
    </fr:p><fr:p>
        The forward directions are trivial.
        Suppose <fr:tex>(F, U, (π_{a, x})_{a ∈ A, x ∈ X})</fr:tex> extends to <fr:tex>\overline {F} ⊣_π \overline {U}</fr:tex>.
        Then <fr:link
href="subb-000E.xml"
type="local"
addr="subb-000E">I</fr:link> expresses that <fr:tex>∀g : A(a, b). ∀f : A(F(x), a).         π_{x, b}(g∘f) = \overline {U}(g)∘π_{a, x}(f)</fr:tex>. This is exactly the condition
        that <fr:tex>∀g : A(a, b). π</fr:tex> is natural in <fr:tex>a</fr:tex> at <fr:tex>g</fr:tex>, which is certainly
        true of an adjunction.
        <fr:link
href="subbb-000E.xml"
type="local"
addr="subbb-000E">II</fr:link> expresses something similar (dual).
    </fr:p><fr:p>
        The nontrivial directions are the backward directions.
    </fr:p><fr:ul><fr:li><fr:p>
                (I) wts I <fr:tex>⟹ \overline {U}</fr:tex> is a functor.
                Suppose <fr:tex>∀a,b ∈ A. ∀x ∈ X.</fr:tex> I holds.
                Then in particular, <fr:tex>∀c ∈ A. </fr:tex>I holds at <fr:tex>x = U(c)</fr:tex>.
            </fr:p>
            
        <fr:embedded-tex
hash="e29a45e5c3dfdeb85542e204370f1350"><fr:embedded-tex-preamble>\usepackage {tikz-cd}\usepackage {unicode-math}</fr:embedded-tex-preamble><fr:embedded-tex-body>
            \begin {tikzcd}
                A(a, b)×A(FUc, a) \ar [r, "\overline {U}×π_{a,Uc}"]\ar [d, "∘"] &amp; X(Ua, Ub)×X(Uc, Ua) \ar [d, "∘"] \\
                A(FUc, b) \ar [r, "π_{Uc, b}"] &amp; X(Uc, Ub)
            \end {tikzcd}
        </fr:embedded-tex-body></fr:embedded-tex>

            <fr:p>
                As does this trivial square:
            </fr:p>
            <fr:embedded-tex
hash="09f36af7f81c413359f103936dde5f36"><fr:embedded-tex-preamble>\usepackage {tikz-cd}\usepackage {unicode-math}</fr:embedded-tex-preamble><fr:embedded-tex-body>
                \begin {tikzcd}
                    A(a, b)×A(c, a) \ar [r, "id×(-∘ϵ_c)"]\ar [d, "∘"] &amp; A(a, b)×A(FUc, a) \ar [d, "∘"] \\
                    A(c, b) \ar [r, "(-∘ϵ_c)"] &amp; A(FUc, b)
                \end {tikzcd}
            </fr:embedded-tex-body></fr:embedded-tex>
            <fr:p>
                which when pasted together, tells us magically:
            </fr:p>
            <fr:embedded-tex
hash="4de6c4a2a2cb4b6f0547069c1ccdc586"><fr:embedded-tex-preamble>\usepackage {tikz-cd}\usepackage {unicode-math}</fr:embedded-tex-preamble><fr:embedded-tex-body>
                \begin {tikzcd}
                    A(a, b)×A(c, a) \ar [r, "\overline {U}×\overline {U}"]\ar [d, "∘"] &amp; X(Ua, Ub)×X(Uc, Ua) \ar [d, "∘"] \\
                    A(c, b) \ar [r, "\overline {U}"] &amp; X(Uc, Ub)
                \end {tikzcd}
            </fr:embedded-tex-body></fr:embedded-tex>
            <fr:p>
                Combined with <fr:link
href="hejohns-000D.xml"
type="local"
addr="hejohns-000D">the earlier proof</fr:link> that <fr:tex>\overline {U}(1ₐ)                 = 1_{\overline {U}(a)}</fr:tex> (which just follows by definition),
                we've shown that <fr:tex>\overline {U}</fr:tex> defines a functorial action on morphisms.
                Actually, we saw earlier that <fr:link
href="subb-000E.xml"
type="local"
addr="subb-000E">I</fr:link> expresses that
                <fr:tex>π</fr:tex> is natural in <fr:tex>a</fr:tex>, so we've met the preconditions of <fr:link
href="hejohns-0007.xml"
type="local"
addr="hejohns-0007">the
                precursor lemma</fr:link>, thus there is a unique
                extension <fr:tex>\overline {F}</fr:tex> st <fr:tex>\overline {F} ⊣_π \overline {U}</fr:tex>.
            </fr:p>
            <fr:p>
                There's two ways ways to see the uniqueness situation:
            </fr:p>
            <fr:ul><fr:li>
                    (An intermediate notion) <fr:tex>\overline {U}</fr:tex> is the unique extension of
                    <fr:tex>U</fr:tex> to a functor st <fr:tex>π</fr:tex> is natural in <fr:tex>a</fr:tex>.
                    We've essentially seen this already, if not literally.
                    For any functor <fr:tex>U'</fr:tex>, naturality in <fr:tex>a</fr:tex> requires the following square to hold for all <fr:tex>g : A(a, b)</fr:tex>:
                    <fr:embedded-tex
hash="2811be9da07c73dde4ea7d726bca767c"><fr:embedded-tex-preamble>\usepackage {tikz-cd}\usepackage {unicode-math}</fr:embedded-tex-preamble><fr:embedded-tex-body>
                        \begin {tikzcd}
                            A(FU'a, a) \ar [r, "g∘-"] &amp; A(FU'a, b) \ar [d, "≀"', "π"] \\
                            X(U'a, U'a) \ar [r, "U'g∘-"]\ar [u, "≀"', "π⁻¹"] &amp; X(U'a, U'b)
                        \end {tikzcd}
                    </fr:embedded-tex-body></fr:embedded-tex>
                    Thus, <fr:tex>∀g. U'(g) = \overline {U}(g)</fr:tex>.
                    Then, with <fr:tex>\overline {U}</fr:tex> a functor and <fr:tex>π</fr:tex> natural in <fr:tex>a</fr:tex>, <fr:link
href="hejohns-0007.xml"
type="local"
addr="hejohns-0007">the
                    precursor</fr:link> gives us a unique <fr:tex>\overline {F}</fr:tex> st <fr:tex>\overline {F}                     ⊣ \overline {U}</fr:tex> uniquely.
                </fr:li>
                <fr:li>
                    (Going backwards) Since the definitions of <fr:tex>\overline {F}</fr:tex> and
                    <fr:tex>\overline {U}</fr:tex> (in <fr:link
href="hejohns-0007.xml"
type="local"
addr="hejohns-0007">the precursor</fr:link>) are not dependent
                    on the other functor, the total adjunction <fr:tex>\overline {F} ⊣ \overline {U}</fr:tex> is
                    the unique one extending the core by applying the
                    uniqueness result of <fr:link
href="hejohns-0007.xml"
type="local"
addr="hejohns-0007">the precusor</fr:link> to each
                    functor in turn, just knowing the other is a well-defined
                    functor.
                </fr:li></fr:ul></fr:li>
        <fr:li><fr:p>
                (II) wts II <fr:tex>⟹ \overline {F}</fr:tex> is a functor.
                This is dual.
            </fr:p></fr:li></fr:ul></fr:mainmatter></fr:tree><fr:tree
